WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.633
K-Means.

2
00:00:01.933 --> 00:00:04.429
Sometimes nobody has labeled your data.

3
00:00:04.429 --> 00:00:06.733
K-Means finds the groups on its own.

4
00:00:07.933 --> 00:00:09.767
The recipe is wonderfully short.

5
00:00:10.067 --> 00:00:12.867
Pick k centroids, the centers of your clusters.

6
00:00:13.167 --> 00:00:16.833
Then alternate two steps: assign points, and move centroids.

7
00:00:17.133 --> 00:00:18.900
Stop when nothing moves any more.

8
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:21.100
Here is the loop.

9
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:23.667
Assign every point to its nearest centroid.

10
00:00:23.967 --> 00:00:27.367
Then move each centroid to the mean of the points assigned to it.

11
00:00:27.667 --> 00:00:29.744
Repeat until nothing changes.

12
00:00:29.744 --> 00:00:31.033
It converges fast.

13
00:00:32.233 --> 00:00:33.867
But you must choose k yourself.

14
00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:39.133
For each k we measure inertia, the total squared distance from points to their centroids.

15
00:00:39.433 --> 00:00:42.765
Look for the elbow, where the curve stops dropping steeply.

16
00:00:42.765 --> 00:00:44.233
Here, that is around four.

17
00:00:45.433 --> 00:00:47.000
Here it is in scikit-learn.

18
00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:49.267
First we standardize the features.

19
00:00:49.567 --> 00:00:52.967
Then we try every k from one to ten and print the inertia.

20
00:00:53.267 --> 00:00:56.433
After spotting the elbow, we refit with four clusters.

21
00:00:57.633 --> 00:01:01.900
One classic mistake: feeding raw features with wildly different units.

22
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:05.537
Standardize first, because K-Means is pure distance.

23
00:01:05.537 --> 00:01:09.900
And remember, it only finds round clusters, not crescents or chains.

24
00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:12.400
Now a real example.

25
00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:18.400
Cluster the exoplanet table on period, radius, and temperature, with k equal to four.

26
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:25.900
Out come the families astronomers already name: hot Jupiters, warm Neptunes, super-Earths, and long-period giants.

27
00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:29.333
Nobody told the algorithm those categories exist.

28
00:01:30.533 --> 00:01:31.327
Let's recap.

29
00:01:31.327 --> 00:01:35.033
K-Means assigns points and moves centroids until stable.

30
00:01:35.333 --> 00:01:37.333
You choose k, and the elbow helps.

31
00:01:37.633 --> 00:01:40.367
Standardize first, and expect round clusters.

32
00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:42.333
Next up: DBSCAN.
